TASK:{"title":"SWEN3204 Software Architecture – Assignment 2: Short-Answer Questions","overview":"Answer six short questions applying architecture concepts to the CampusEats scenario. The assignment is worth 40 marks, including 4 marks for communication. Submit an individual, single-document response."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Q1 Quality attributes","weight":null,"max":6,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":5,"max":6,"desc":"Two attributes that clearly matter most are chosen and justified from the scenario. Both scenarios have all six parts and both response measures are measurable."},{"label":"Proficient","min":3,"max":4,"desc":"Suitable attributes are chosen with some justification. Scenarios are mostly complete but one is missing a part or has a response measure that is not measurable."},{"label":"Developing","min":1,"max":2,"desc":"Attributes are named with little or no justification, or the scenarios are not in six-part form or are not measurable."},{"label":"Missing","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"Not attempted or irrelevant."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Q2 Architectural style","weight":null,"max":6,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":5,"max":6,"desc":"A clear recommendation justified by at least two trade-offs that are specific to the scenario (team size, load profile, known problems). The downside of the recommendation is acknowledged."},{"label":"Proficient","min":3,"max":4,"desc":"A clear recommendation with two trade-offs, but one is generic or not linked to the scenario, or no downside is acknowledged."},{"label":"Developing","min":1,"max":2,"desc":"A recommendation with only one trade-off, or with generic textbook advantages and disadvantages only."},{"label":"Missing","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"Not attempted or no recommendation."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Q3 Availability calculation","weight":null,"max":8,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":7,"max":8,"desc":"Parts (a), (b) and (c) are all correct with working shown, to the requested precision."},{"label":"Proficient","min":5,"max":6,"desc":"The method is correct throughout but there is one arithmetic or rounding slip, or exactly one part is incorrect."},{"label":"Developing","min":1,"max":4,"desc":"The series calculation is attempted but two or more parts are incorrect, or working is not shown."},{"label":"Missing","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"Not attempted or no correct element."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Q4 Resilience pattern","weight":null,"max":5,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":5,"max":5,"desc":"An appropriate pattern is named, its mechanism is explained correctly, it is applied to the outage, and at least one drawback or tuning consideration is given."},{"label":"Proficient","min":3,"max":4,"desc":"An appropriate pattern is named and explained correctly, but it is only loosely applied to the outage or no drawback or tuning consideration is given."},{"label":"Developing","min":1,"max":2,"desc":"A pattern is named but the explanation is incorrect or very thin."},{"label":"Missing","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"Not attempted or an unsuitable pattern."}]},{"id":"C5","name":"Q5 Consistency and availability","weight":null,"max":6,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":5,"max":6,"desc":"Both choices are justified, the CAP theorem is used correctly, and a suitable concrete technique is named for each case."},{"label":"Proficient","min":3,"max":4,"desc":"Both choices are reasonable and techniques are named, but the CAP theorem is explained incorrectly or imprecisely, or one technique is missing."},{"label":"Developing","min":1,"max":2,"desc":"Choices are stated with little justification, or the CAP theorem is not used."},{"label":"Missing","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"Not attempted."}]},{"id":"C6","name":"Q6 Architecture decision record","weight":null,"max":5,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":5,"max":5,"desc":"Context, Decision and Consequences are all present and specific to the scenario, with at least one positive and one negative consequence."},{"label":"Proficient","min":3,"max":4,"desc":"All three sections are present but the consequences are one-sided (no negative consequence) or the context is generic."},{"label":"Developing","min":1,"max":2,"desc":"One or more sections are missing or the record does not describe a decision."},{"label":"Missing","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"Not attempted."}]},{"id":"C7","name":"Communication and format","weight":null,"max":4,"levels":[{"label":"Excellent","min":4,"max":4,"desc":"Every answer is within 150 words, terminology is precise, the required headings are used and sources are cited in IEEE style."},{"label":"Proficient","min":3,"max":3,"desc":"Clear writing, but one answer exceeds the word limit or there are minor citation or formatting problems."},{"label":"Developing","min":1,"max":2,"desc":"Several answers exceed the word limit, or the writing is hard to follow, or sources are not cited."},{"label":"Missing","min":0,"max":0,"desc":"Format requirements ignored."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"partial","where":"Question 1"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Question 2"},{"id":"R3","met":"partial","where":"Question 3"},{"id":"R4","met":"partial","where":"Question 4"},{"id":"R5","met":"partial","where":"Question 5"},{"id":"R6","met":"partial","where":"Question 6"},{"id":"R7","met":"no","where":"References"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Question 3","issue":"Part (c) gives payment-system availability (99.9975%) as end-to-end order-placement availability. Multiplying by the other required component availabilities gives approximately 99.80% end-to-end availability and 1.5 hours monthly downtime."},{"where":"Question 4","issue":"Circuit Breaker state transitions are reversed: Closed permits calls, failures transition to Open, and Half-Open permits trial calls."},{"where":"Question 5","issue":"CAP is misstated. During a partition, a system must trade off consistency and availability; it cannot choose consistency and availability while giving up partition tolerance."},{"where":"Question 5","issue":"No concrete consistency technique is named for placing and paying for an order."},{"where":"Question 6","issue":"Consequences omit a negative consequence of introducing the queue."},{"where":"References","issue":"Citation [2] is used but has no corresponding reference-list entry, and no assumptions are stated."}],"overall":"Total: 21/40. The recommendation and most calculations are sound, but the answer has important errors in the circuit-breaker states, CAP explanation, and end-to-end redundancy calculation. The availability scenario is not measurable, and citations and ADR consequences are incomplete.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Developing","mark":2.0,"scale":6.0,"points":2.0,"max":6.0,"reasoning":"Performance and availability are justified using the scenario, and the performance scenario has six parts with measurable response measures. The availability scenario's measure is not measurable, and its response does not clearly address payment failure.","evidence":[{"where":"Question 1","quote":"Response measure: the system should stay up most of the time."}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Excellent","mark":5.0,"scale":6.0,"points":5.0,"max":6.0,"reasoning":"The modular-monolith recommendation is clear and tied to team size, existing performance and deployment problems, and the 12-month horizon. It gives multiple scenario-specific trade-offs and acknowledges a downside.","evidence":[{"where":"Question 2","quote":"With five developers, running eight or ten services would mean more time spent on infrastructure than on features."},{"where":"Question 2","quote":"The trade-off I accept is that modules cannot be scaled independently, and a bug in one module can still crash the whole process."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Proficient","mark":5.0,"scale":8.0,"points":5.0,"max":8.0,"reasoning":"Parts (a) and (b) are essentially correct to the requested precision. In (c), the redundant payment availability is calculated correctly, but it is not combined with the other required components; therefore end-to-end availability and downtime are wrong.","evidence":[{"where":"Question 3","quote":"A = 0.9995 × 0.999 × 0.9995 × 0.995 = 0.99301"},{"where":"Question 3","quote":"Downtime = (1 − 0.99301) × 720 = 0.00699 × 720 = 3.5 hours per month."},{"where":"Question 3","quote":"So the new availability of placing an order is 99.9975%"}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Developing","mark":2.0,"scale":5.0,"points":2.0,"max":5.0,"reasoning":"The Circuit Breaker is appropriate and its intended application is clear, but its states are explained incorrectly: normal traffic passes in Closed, failures open the breaker, and trial calls occur in Half-Open. The outage discussion does not correct this mechanism error.","evidence":[{"where":"Question 4","quote":"When failures exceed a threshold (for example 50% of calls in 10 seconds) the breaker moves to Closed"}]},{"id":"C5","level":"Developing","mark":2.0,"scale":6.0,"points":2.0,"max":6.0,"reasoning":"The menu choice is justified with a concrete cache technique. The payment choice invokes CAP incorrectly: during a partition, a system cannot guarantee both consistency and availability. No concrete payment technique is named.","evidence":[{"where":"Question 5","quote":"for payments we choose consistency and availability and give up partition tolerance."}]},{"id":"C6","level":"Proficient","mark":3.0,"scale":5.0,"points":3.0,"max":5.0,"reasoning":"Context, Decision, and Consequences are present and specific. The consequences are only positive; no negative consequence such as eventual delivery, duplicate handling, or queue-operation overhead is acknowledged.","evidence":[{"where":"Question 6","quote":"Consequences: Placing an order no longer waits for the outlet, so the response time improves."}]},{"id":"C7","level":"Developing","mark":2.0,"scale":4.0,"points":2.0,"max":4.0,"reasoning":"The required question headings and order are used, and answers appear within the word limit. Citation and format requirements are incomplete: reference [2] is missing, and assumptions are not stated.","evidence":[{"where":"References","quote":"[1] L. Bass, P. Clements, and R. Kazman, *Software Architecture in Practice*, 3rd ed. Upper Saddle River, NJ, USA: Addison-Wesley, 2012."},{"where":"Question 4","quote":"The Circuit Breaker pattern [2] would have contained the failure."}]}]}
